  "lean": {
    "statement": "∀ {E : Type u_1} [inst : NormedAddCommGroup E] [inst_1 : InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [inst_2 : FiniteDimensional ℝ E]\n  [inst_3 : MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E] [SecondCountableTopology E] (f : E → ℝ),\n  Differentiable ℝ f →\n    ∀ (eta beta : ℝ),\n      0 < eta →\n        ∀ (hbeta : 0 ≤ beta),\n          LipschitzWith ⟨beta, hbeta⟩ (gradient f) →\n            ∀ (h xp : E),\n              have P := MeasureTheory.Measure.map (fun z => √eta • z) (ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E);\n              have U := MeasureTheory.MeasureSpace.volume.restrict (Set.Ioc 0 1);\n              have C := ∫ (z : E), f (h + z) ∂P - Inner.inner ℝ (gradient f xp) h;\n              √eta ^ 2 = eta ∧\n                MeasureTheory.IsProbabilityMeasure U ∧\n                  MeasureTheory.Integrable (fun z => f (h + z)) P ∧\n                    ∀ (x : E),\n                      (Measurable fun q =>\n                          Inner.inner ℝ\n                            ((Real.pi / 2) •\n                              (Real.cos (Real.pi / 2 * q.1) • (x - h) - Real.sin (Real.pi / 2 * q.1) • q.2))\n                            (gradient f xp -\n                              gradient f\n                                (h + Real.sin (Real.pi / 2 * q.1) • (x - h) + Real.cos (Real.pi / 2 * q.1) • q.2))) ∧\n                        MeasureTheory.Integrable\n                            (fun q =>\n                              Inner.inner ℝ\n                                ((Real.pi / 2) •\n                                  (Real.cos (Real.pi / 2 * q.1) • (x - h) - Real.sin (Real.pi / 2 * q.1) • q.2))\n                                (gradient f xp -\n                                  gradient f\n                                    (h + Real.sin (Real.pi / 2 * q.1) • (x - h) + Real.cos (Real.pi / 2 * q.1) • q.2)))\n                            (U.prod P) ∧\n                          (∀ (z : E),\n                              ∫ (r : ℝ) in 0..1,\n                                  Inner.inner ℝ\n                                    ((Real.pi / 2) •\n                                      (Real.cos (Real.pi / 2 * r) • (x - h) - Real.sin (Real.pi / 2 * r) • z))\n                                    (gradient f xp -\n                                      gradient f\n                                        (h + Real.sin (Real.pi / 2 * r) • (x - h) + Real.cos (Real.pi / 2 * r) • z)) =\n                                Inner.inner ℝ (gradient f xp) (x - (h + z)) - f x + f (h + z)) ∧\n                            ∫ (q : ℝ × E),\n                                Inner.inner ℝ\n                                  ((Real.pi / 2) •\n                                    (Real.cos (Real.pi / 2 * q.1) • (x - h) - Real.sin (Real.pi / 2 * q.1) • q.2))\n                                  (gradient f xp -\n                                    gradient f\n                                      (h + Real.sin (Real.pi / 2 * q.1) • (x - h) +\n                                        Real.cos (Real.pi / 2 * q.1) • q.2)) ∂U.prod P =\n                              Inner.inner ℝ (gradient f xp) x - f x + C",
    "statement_sha256": "5a1f72f6cafcd91f370654a19262139161cac6294af049640378fa5ae4003288",
    "compiled": true,
    "approved_definition_context": [
      "stdGaussian E is the centered identity-covariance Gaussian probability measure on E. Measure.map is pushforward and Measure.prod is the independent product.",
      "volume is Lebesgue measure on the real line. Measure.restrict restricts a measure to the given set. Set.Ioc 0 1 is the interval (0,1]. IsProbabilityMeasure means total mass one.",
      "gradient f is the real inner-product gradient; Differentiable means differentiability at every point. LipschitzWith beta bounds distances by beta times input distance.",
      "All integrals are real or Bochner integrals as typed. Integrable asserts Bochner integrability. The interval integral from 0 to 1 uses the standard oriented interval-integral convention.",
      "All three local estimator/path definitions have been expanded from the compiled theorem type and Lean checked definitional equality. Let-bound constants retain their displayed quantifier scope."
    ]
  },
